 BFS 种子，以 4 邻域、\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:24:#     等速、同时向外扩散；先到达的种子占领该格，形成曼哈顿 Voronoi 式分区。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:26:#   - 例：一个 7x7 房间，左上角有红种子，右下角有绿种子。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:28:#   - 变体：每个格子比较它到红种子、绿种子的曼哈顿距离，离谁近就染谁；\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:31:#   - 若大量区域只有 0-1 个种子，输出会退化成纯色块，BFS 竞争不明显。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:32:#   - 因此生成器强制至少 1 个区域放置 3 个种子，并偏向 2-3 种子区域。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:33:#   - 种子位置从少量可见模板中选取（角点、边中点、中心），避免难以肉眼归纳的随意散点。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:38:#     变体中每个标记都是独立种子，即使深度相同也不能合并。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:40:#     只取决于边界深度。变体里目标颜色改为“最先到达的种子颜色”，必须逐步扩散赋值。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:42:#   - 区域仍由递归矩形切分得到，因此每个 bbox 就是真实房间，可直接在 bbox 的模板位置放种子。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:46:#   - tie-break 采用可见的“种子阅读顺序（从上到下、从左到右）”；但生成器主要用不对称模板，\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:24:#    - D1/2：孤立彩色像素不再是调色板槽位，而是 4 邻域多源 BFS 的种子。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:26:#    小例子：一个 8x8 房间里，左上/右上/左下有三个单像素种子，中部偏右有一个 2x3\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:28:#    “绕障碍的 Voronoi”，右上的颜色不能直穿阻挡块，部分区域改由其余种子绕行占领。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:30:#    只做 BFS 而没有障碍时，视觉上容易退化成普通距离分区；只做障碍而种子太少时，\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:32:#    - 至少 1 个区域含 3 个种子，保证竞争边界明显；\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:33:#    - 至少 1 个带阻挡的区域同时也是多种子竞争区域；\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:39:#      单像素连通分量 = 种子，多像素连通分量 = 阻挡物，否则会把障碍误当颜色线索。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:41:#      只能在非阻挡自由格上进行 BFS，且未被任何种子到达的格子保留 base。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:43:#    生成器仍通过递归矩形切分得到 3-6 个房间。种子只放在模板锚点上，阻挡块只放在\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:44:#    房间内部、距边界至少 1 格的位置，不与墙、其他阻挡物或种子重叠。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:46:#    解题者可以直接看到三类角色：墙、孤立单像素种子、紧凑多像素阻挡块。输出中的\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:49:#    每个房间至多一个阻挡块，种子位置从少量模板中选取，区域数量也受限，避免一张图\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/3/code.py:52:#    颜色编号本身不参与任何隐藏排序。颜色只表示“哪一个种子”以及“哪一块是障碍”；\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/2/code.py:10:#      输入里 active 区域的 marker 放在该螺旋前缀的连续几个格子上；前两个 marker 强制同色，形成可见的入口种子。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/5/code.py:47:#    generator 对每个候选样本同时计算：\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:21:#     的整张画布外边缘上，并直接充当 BFS 种子。距离从标记本身开始算，多个种子同时以\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:22:#     4 邻域向区域内部扩散，谁先到谁占领该格，平局按种子阅读顺序打破。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:25:#     顶边往下扩散的三块竞争区域，分界线由到各种子的曼哈顿距离决定。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:30:#   - 若大多数区域只有 0-1 个种子，输出会退化成纯色覆盖，看不出多源 BFS 的竞争。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:31:#   - 生成器因此强制至少一个边缘区域拥有 3 个种子，并尽量再让另一个边缘区域拥有 2 个\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:32:#     种子；其余区域仍允许 0-3 个种子，保证“有竞争”和“有无变化对照”同时出现。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:33:#   - 种子位置只从少量边缘锚点模板中选择（边角与边中点），避免随意散点导致人眼难以\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:40:#     都是独立种子，即使它们同在边缘、同在深度 0，也必须同时保留并竞争空间。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:42:#     最近种子是谁，再取决于 BFS 到达顺序，必须显式做多源扩散。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:51:#     自动进入“无种子、保持底色”的分支。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:58:#   - 输出不再是边界同心框，而是从边缘种子长出来的 Voronoi 式色块；与原题视觉差异显著。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:62:#   - 每个区域最多 3 个种子，且只落在边角/边中等稀疏位置，输入仍然整洁。\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:68:#   - `base = _mode(vals)` 假设底色在单个区域内仍占多数；生成器将每区种子数限制在 0-3。\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed56627177/_codegen_logs/run_20260618_210621_gpt54_high_missing/12__codegen_prompts__7.log:65:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_33\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/29/code.py\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Combo variant of b9e38dc0 (index 29)\\n# combo_spec: D12/0+D13/1\\n# Name: 统一开口方向+颜色编码方向\\n\\n# Design analysis\\n# ---------------\\n# spec:\\n#   fused_variant:\\n#     summary: |\\n#       以 D13/1 的“闭合门面颜色编码开口”作为基础，再叠加 D12/0 的“同图多个精灵且方向统一”。\\n#       每个精灵仍是原题家族里的漏斗/半包围墙，但原本的几何缺口被一条直线门面补上；\\n#       门面颜色不再只是“这是门”，而是从固定色表里编码全局辐射方向。\\n#       同一张图至少有两个精灵，所有精灵的门面颜色相同，因此它们共享同一 outward direction。\\n#   concrete_geometry:\\n#     sprite:\\n#       - 保留原题的两条锯齿臂 + 闭合端 + 1:1 / 1:2 两种臂角模式。\\n#       - 在原开口处补一条整齐的水平/垂直门面，长度仍为 gap+1。\\n#       - 非门面的墙统一使用 closed_color；门面使用 direction_color。\\n#     multiplicity:\\n#       - 每张图放置 2 个精灵，彼此独立、互不重叠，并沿与辐射垂直的轴分开摆放。\\n#       - 因为两个精灵共享相同门面色，所以会重复支撑“这个颜色代表这个方向”。\\n#     palette:\\n#       - 方向编码色固定为 4 个显眼颜色：1/2/3/4 分别对应 上/右/下/左。\\n#       - 背景、闭合墙、填充种子、障碍全部避开这 4 个方向色，避免视觉混淆。\\n#   behavior_trace:\\n#     example: |\\n#       例：若门面颜色为 2（红），则两只闭合漏斗都朝右辐射。\\n#       原题流程：从真实缺口的两个端点推出开口方向，再向外放射。\\n#       现在流程：solver 先识别所有长直线门面，读出它们的颜色 2 -> 右；\\n#       然后对每个精灵，把这条线视作“可穿透门”，从门两端恢复边界射线，\\n#       内部填充穿门而出，继续向右扩散；障碍仍沿同方向制造阴影。\\n#     difference: |\\n#       如果还按原题把“缺失的一面”当作开口，则这里根本没有缺口；\\n#       如果只做单个 D13/1 精灵，也看不出 D12/0 的“统一全局方向重复出现”。\\n#   complexity_calibration:\\n#     - 保留原题的锯齿臂、两类射线斜率、阴影机制，所以仍属于同一视觉家族。\\n#     - 只增加一个低自由度的符号层：4 个固定方向色；同图内只激活其中 1 个，便于少样例归纳。\\n#     - 精灵数固定为 2，既能体现“多个且统一”，又不会把辐射区挤得过于混乱。\\n#   implicit_assumptions_review:\\n#     - 原代码假设 wall 是单色且唯一主组件；这里改为 closed_color 最大、open_color 为多段直线门面。\\n#     - 原代码依赖 degree-1 端点来找开口；这里墙体闭合，必须改为读取门面两端。\\n#     - 原代码默认墙完全不可穿过；这里 open_color 必须放入可通行集合，但输出保留原色。\\n#     - 原代码按单组件处理；这里要遍历多个 wall component，并给每个组件单独加外放射边界。\\n#     - 新增假设：所有门面都用同一个方向色，且每个门面是长度 >= 6 的单一直线段。\\n#       generator 通过固定门面构造、统一色表和障碍配色限制来保证这些前提成立。\\n#   placement_legality:\\n#     - 组件按与辐射垂直的轴分槽放置，保证输入时 bbox 不重叠。\\n#     - 同时要求门面投影区间留足空隙，降低多组件辐射区在输出中相互缠绕的概率。\\n#     - 障碍只放在背景格、远离墙体，并基于无障碍时的 nominal fill zone 采样。\\n#   observability_and_cleanliness:\\n#     - 人类会看到：同一种门面色在同图重复出现，两个精灵都朝同一方向出流。\\n#     - 方向色不复用于背景/种子/障碍，因此“颜色是语义标签”肉眼可见。\\n#     - 两个精灵分开摆放，门面是整齐直线，障碍仍是稀疏点状，层次不会打架。\\n\\nimport random\\n\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom scipy.ndimage import distance_transform_cdt\\n\\nfrom arc_utils import components_mask, get_bg, neighbors4, neighbors8\\n\\n\\nDIR_TO_COLOR = {\\n    (-1, 0): 1,  # blue -> up\\n    (0, 1): 2,   # red -> right\\n    (1, 0): 3,   # green -> down\\n    (0, -1): 4,  # yellow -> left\\n}\\nCOLOR_TO_DIR = {color: direction for direction, color in DIR_TO_COLOR.items()}\\nNON_DIRECTION_COLORS = [0, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]\\n\\n\\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Fill several closed funnels and let a shared door color dictate their common exit direction.\\n\\n    Input structure:\\n    - The background is the dominant color. Each sprite is a closed funnel: most wall pixels use one closed-wall color, while one straight wall face uses a direction-coded color.\\n    - There are multiple such sprites in one grid, and all coded faces share the same color, so every sprite uses the same outward direction. Small fill blobs sit inside the funnels; other sparse colors are obstacles.\\n\\n    Rule:\\n    - Decode the common outward direction from the door-face color, then treat each coded face as permeable while the rest of the wall stays solid.\\n    - Flood-fill from the interior seeds through each coded face, extend the fill outward within the two recovered boundary rays, and apply obstacle shadows along that shared direction.\\n\\n    Key insight: Geometry now tells you where each door segment is, but the door color tells you which global direction all sprites radiate toward; once that color is decoded, the same direction applies to every funnel in the grid.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    g = np.array(grid, copy=True)\\n    h, w = g.shape\\n\\n    vals = np.unique(g)\\n    bg = get_bg(g)\\n    non_bg = [int(v) for v in vals if int(v) != bg]\\n    assert non_bg, \\\"expected at least one non-background color\\\"\\n\\n    def sgn(x: int) -> int:\\n        return (x > 0) - (x < 0)\\n\\n    closed_color = max(non_bg, key=lambda c: int((g == c).sum()))\\n\\n    def straight_segments_for_color(color: int) -> list[set[tuple[int, int]]]:\\n        segments: list[set[tuple[int, int]]] = []\\n        for cells in components_mask(g == color, connectivity=4):\\n            pts = {(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in cells}\\n            if len(pts) < 6:\\n                continue\\n            rows = {r for r, _ in pts}\\n            cols = {c for _, c in pts}\\n            if len(rows) == 1:\\n                row = next(iter(rows))\\n                c0 = min(c for _, c in pts)\\n                c1 = max(c for _, c in pts)\\n                expected = {(row, cc) for cc in range(c0, c1 + 1)}\\n                endpoints = [(row, c0), (row, c1)]\\n            elif len(cols) == 1:\\n                col = next(iter(cols))\\n                r0 = min(r for r, _ in pts)\\n                r1 = max(r for r, _ in pts)\\n                expected = {(rr, col) for rr in range(r0, r1 + 1)}\\n                endpoints = [(r0, col), (r1, col)]\\n            else:\\n                continue\\n            if pts != expected:\\n                continue\\n            ok = True\\n            for ep in endpoints:\\n                touches_closed = any(\\n                    0 <= rr < h and 0 <= cc < w and g[rr, cc] == closed_color\\n                    for rr, cc in neighbors8(*ep)\\n                )\\n                if not touches_closed:\\n                    ok = False\\n                    break\\n            if ok:\\n                segments.append(pts)\\n        return segments\\n\\n    open_color = None\\n    open_segments: list[set[tuple[int, int]]] = []\\n    best_score = None\\n    for color in non_bg:\\n        if color == closed_color or color not in COLOR_TO_DIR:\\n            continue\\n        segments = straight_segments_for_color(color)\\n        if len(segments) < 2:\\n            continue\\n        score = (sum(len(seg) for seg in segments), len(segments))\\n        if best_score is None or score > best_score:\\n            best_score = score\\n            open_color = color\\n            open_segments = segments\\n\\n    assert open_color is not None, \\\"expected repeated straight door faces in a direction color\\\"\\n    rad = COLOR_TO_DIR[open_color]\\n\\n    wall_mask = (g == closed_color) | (g == open_color)\\n    wall_comps = [set((int(r), int(c)) for r, c in pts) for pts in components_mask(wall_mask, connectivity=8)]\\n    assert len(wall_comps) >= 2, \\\"expected multiple wall components\\\"\\n\\n    other_colors = [c for c in non_bg if c not in (closed_color, open_color)]\\n    assert other_colors, \\\"expected at least one non-wall color\\\"\\n    wall_points = [p for comp in wall_comps for p in comp]\\n\\n    def min_dist_to_wall(color: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\\n        pts = [(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in np.argwhere(g == color)]\\n        best_d = 10**9\\n        for r, c in pts:\\n            for wr, wc in wall_points:\\n                d = abs(r - wr) + abs(c - wc)\\n                if d < best_d:\\n                    best_d = d\\n                    if best_d == 0:\\n                        return 0, len(pts)\\n        return best_d, len(pts)\\n\\n    fill_color = min(other_colors, key=lambda c: (min_dist_to_wall(c)[0], min_dist_to_wall(c)[1]))\\n    fill_pos = [(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in np.argwhere(g == fill_color)]\\n    if not fill_pos:\\n        return g\\n\\n    barrier = g == closed_color\\n\\n    def comp_neighbors(comp_mask: np.ndarray, r: int, c: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\\n        out = []\\n        for rr, cc in neighbors8(r, c):\\n            if 0 <= rr < h and 0 <= cc < w and comp_mask[rr, cc]:\\n                out.append((rr, cc))\\n        return out\\n\\n    def add_ray(start: tuple[int, int], d: tuple[int, int]) -> None:\\n        rr, cc = start\\n        while True:\\n            rr += d[0]\\n            cc += d[1]\\n            if not (0 <= rr < h and 0 <= cc < w):\\n                break\\n            barrier[rr, cc] = True\\n\\n    def add_ray_12(start: tuple[int, int], d: tuple[int, int]) -> None:\\n        rr, cc = start\\n        step = 0\\n        while True:\\n            step += 1\\n            if step % 2 == 1:\\n                rr += d[0]\\n                cc += d[1]\\n            else:\\n                if rad[0] != 0:\\n                    rr += d[0]\\n                else:\\n                    cc += d[1]\\n            if not (0 <= rr < h and 0 <= cc < w):\\n                break\\n            barrier[rr, cc] = True\\n\\n    assigned_segments = 0\\n    for wall_comp in wall_comps:\\n        comp_open_segments = [seg for seg in open_segments if seg <= wall_comp]\\n        assert len(comp_open_segments) == 1, \\\"each wall component must contain exactly one coded face\\\"\\n        assigned_segments += 1\\n        open_face = comp_open_segments[0]\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed165869686/_codegen_logs/continue_20260619_141311/10_6.log:31:<new_puzzle index=\"6\">\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed56627177/_codegen_logs/run_20260618_210621_gpt54_high_missing/1__codegen_prompts__5.log:20:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/e8686506.py\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"import numpy as np\\n\\nfrom arc_utils import bbox_coords, components_mask, get_bg, paste, random_free_location\\nimport random\\n\\n\\ndef _piece_sig(pts: np.ndarray) -> tuple[int, int, tuple[tuple[int, int], ...]]:\\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(pts)\\n    coords = tuple(sorted((int(r) - r0, int(c) - c0) for r, c in pts))\\n    return (r1 - r0 + 1), (c1 - c0 + 1), coords\\n\\n\\ndef _factors(n: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\\n    out = []\\n    for w in range(1, n + 1):\\n        if n % w == 0:\\n            h = n // w\\n            out.append((h, w))\\n    return out\\n\\n\\ndef _build_requirements(\\n    H: int,\\n    W: int,\\n    comps_by_color: dict[int, list[list[tuple[int, int]]]],\\n) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Build per-cell required color constraints (req[r,c] == -1 means unconstrained).\\n\\n    Heuristics supported by training examples:\\n      - If there is a color with exactly 4 singleton components, those are the four corners.\\n      - If there are full horizontal bars of length W-2 in the input, use them as the top/bottom edges\\n        between the corners (ordered by their vertical position in the input).\\n      - If there is a color with exactly two full vertical line components (width=1) in the input,\\n        place them on the left/right edges centered vertically.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    req = np.full((H, W), -1, dtype=int)\\n\\n    # 4 singleton components -> corners\\n    corner_colors = [\\n        col\\n        for col, comps in comps_by_color.items()\\n        if len(comps) == 4 and all(len(p) == 1 for p in comps)\\n    ]\\n    assert len(corner_colors) <= 1, \\\"corner singleton color must be unique when present\\\"\\n    corner_color = corner_colors[0] if corner_colors else None\\n    if corner_color is not None:\\n        req[0, 0] = corner_color\\n        req[0, W - 1] = corner_color\\n        req[H - 1, 0] = corner_color\\n        req[H - 1, W - 1] = corner_color\\n\\n    # Horizontal bar(s) of length W-2 (filled, height 1)\\n    bar_colors: list[tuple[int, int]] = []  # (min_input_row, color)\\n    if W >= 3 and corner_color is not None:\\n        for col, comps in comps_by_color.items():\\n            for pts in comps:\\n                r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(pts)\\n                hh, ww = r1 - r0 + 1, c1 - c0 + 1\\n                if hh == 1 and ww == (W - 2) and len(pts) == ww:\\n                    bar_colors.append((r0, col))\\n                    break\\n        if bar_colors:\\n            assert len(bar_colors) <= 2, \\\"horizontal edge bar colors must be top/bottom only\\\"\\n            bar_colors.sort()\\n            top_col = bar_colors[0][1]\\n            bot_col = bar_colors[-1][1]\\n            req[0, 1 : W - 1] = top_col\\n            req[H - 1, 1 : W - 1] = bot_col\\n\\n    # Two full vertical line components -> left/right edges, centered vertically\\n    vertical_line_candidates = []\\n    for col, comps in comps_by_color.items():\\n        if len(comps) != 2:\\n            continue\\n        sigs = [_piece_sig(pts) for pts in comps]\\n        (h0, w0, coords0), (h1, w1, coords1) = sigs[0], sigs[1]\\n        if w0 == 1 and w1 == 1 and h0 == h1 and coords0 == coords1 and h0 > 1:\\n            vertical_line_candidates.append((col, h0))\\n    assert len(vertical_line_candidates) <= 1, \\\"paired vertical edge-line color must be unique when present\\\"\\n    if vertical_line_candidates:\\n        col, L = vertical_line_candidates[0]\\n        top = (H - L) // 2\\n        assert 0 <= top and top + L <= H, \\\"paired vertical edge lines must fit in the output height\\\"\\n        req[top : top + L, 0] = col\\n        req[top : top + L, W - 1] = col\\n\\n    return req\\n\\n\\ndef _assemble(\\n    types: list[dict],\\n    H: int,\\n    W: int,\\n    req: np.ndarray,\\n    disallow_touch: set[int] | None = None,\\n) -> np.ndarray:\\n    board = np.full((H, W), -1, dtype=int)\\n    counts = [int(t[\\\"cnt\\\"]) for t in types]\\n\\n    def mirror_ok(r: int, c: int, col: int) -> bool:\\n        mc = W - 1 - c\\n        v = board[r, mc]\\n        return (v == -1) or (v == col)\\n\\n    def can_place(ti: int, r0: int, c0: int) -> bool:\\n        t = types[ti]\\n        col = int(t[\\\"col\\\"])\\n        is_singleton = len(t[\\\"coords\\\"]) == 1\\n        for dr, dc in t[\\\"coords\\\"]:\\n            r, c = r0 + dr, c0 + dc\\n            if board[r, c] != -1:\\n                return False\\n            if req[r, c] != -1 and int(req[r, c]) != col:\\n                return False\\n            if not mirror_ok(r, c, col):\\n                return False\\n            if is_singleton and not (r == 0 or r == H - 1 or c == 0 or c == W - 1):\\n                return False\\n            if disallow_touch and col in disallow_touch:\\n                for rr, cc in ((r - 1, c), (r + 1, c), (r, c - 1), (r, c + 1)):\\n                    if 0 <= rr < H and 0 <= cc < W and board[rr, cc] == col:\\n                        return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    def place(ti: int, r0: int, c0: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\\n        t = types[ti]\\n        col = int(t[\\\"col\\\"])\\n        changed = []\\n        for dr, dc in t[\\\"coords\\\"]:\\n            r, c = r0 + dr, c0 + dc\\n            board[r, c] = col\\n            changed.append((r, c))\\n        return changed\\n\\n    def unplace(changed: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\\n        for r, c in changed:\\n            board[r, c] = -1\\n\\n    def placements_for(ti: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\\n        t = types[ti]\\n        return [(r0, c0)\\n                for r0 in range(H - int(t[\\\"h\\\"]) + 1)\\n                for c0 in range(W - int(t[\\\"w\\\"]) + 1)\\n                if can_place(ti, r0, c0)]\\n\\n    # Pure constraint propagation: each step, every type must be forced (1 position)\\n    # or batch-forced (positions == cnt). No backtracking. Asserts if stuck.\\n    changed = True\\n    while changed:\\n        changed = False\\n        for ti in range(len(types)):\\n            if counts[ti] <= 0:\\n                continue\\n            places = placements_for(ti)\\n            assert len(places) > 0, \\\"assembly dead end: a piece has no valid placement\\\"\\n            if len(places) == 1:\\n                counts[ti] -= 1\\n                place(ti, places[0][0], places[0][1])\\n                changed = True\\n            elif len(places) == counts[ti]:\\n                for r0, c0 in places:\\n                    counts[ti] -= 1\\n                    place(ti, r0, c0)\\n                changed = True\\n    assert all(c == 0 for c in counts), \\\"assembly not purely propagation-solvable\\\"\\n    assert np.array_equal(board, board[:, ::-1]), \\\"assembled board must be LR-symmetric\\\"\\n    return board\\n\\n\\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Assemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.\\n\\n    Input structure:\\n    - bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.\\n    - Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.\\n\\n    Rule:\\n    - Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.\\n    - Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.\\n    - Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.\\n\\n    Key insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\\n    bg = get_bg(g)\\n\\n    # Extract pieces (connected components per color)\\n    comps_by_color: dict[int, list[list[tuple[int, int]]]] = {}\\n    pieces = []\\n    color_piece_count: dict[int, int] = {}\\n\\n    for col in sorted(set(map(int, np.unique(g)))):\\n        if col == bg:\\n            continue\\n        comps = components_mask(g == col, connectivity=4)\\n        comps_by_color[col] = comps\\n        color_piece_count[col] = len(comps)\\n        for pts in comps:\\n            _, _, coords = _piece_sig(pts)\\n            pieces.append((col, coords))\\n\\n    # Group identical pieces into types\\n    from collections import Counter\\n\\n    ctr = Counter(pieces)\\n    types = []\\n    for (col, coords), cnt in sorted(ctr.items(), key=lambda kv: (-len(kv[0][1]), kv[0][0], kv[0][1])):\\n        arr = np.array(coords, dtype=int)\\n        h = int(arr[:, 0].max() + 1)\\n        w = int(arr[:, 1].max() + 1)\\n        types.append({\\\"col\\\": int(col), \\\"coords\\\": coords, \\\"h\\\": h, \\\"w\\\": w, \\\"cnt\\\": int(cnt)})\\n\\n    total_area = sum(int(t[\\\"cnt\\\"]) * len(t[\\\"coords\\\"]) for t in types)\\n    assert total_area > 0, \\\"total_area must be positive: grid must contain non-background pieces\\\"\\n\\n    # Disallow 4-neigh touching between different pieces of same color (preserve component counts)\\n    # Eval inputs use the unique closest-to-square portrait factor pair as the output shape.\\n    dims = [(h, w) for (h, w) in _factors(total_area) if h >= w]\\n    assert dims, \\\"at least one portrait factor pair must exist for the total piece area\\\"\\n    best_gap = min(abs(h - w) for h, w in dims)\\n    best_dims = [(h, w) for h, w in dims if abs(h - w) == best_gap]\\n    assert len(best_dims) == 1, \\\"closest-to-square portrait output shape must be unique\\\"\\n    H, W = best_dims[0]\\n\\n    # Frame constraint: under 8-connectivity, 1-2 pieces must exist whose union bbox\\n    # equals the output rectangle (H x W). Their relative positions are fixed in output.\\n    comps8_all = []\\n    for col in sorted(set(map(int, np.unique(g))) - {bg}):\\n        for pts in components_mask(g == col, connectivity=8):\\n            comps8_all.append((int(col), pts))\\n    def _is_lr_symmetric(parts, h, w):\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Check if the union of frame parts forms an LR-symmetric bitmap.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        all_pts = np.concatenate([p for _, p in parts])\\n        r0, c0 = int(all_pts[:, 0].min()), int(all_pts[:, 1].min())\\n        bmp = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=bool)\\n        for _, pts in parts:\\n            for r, c in pts:\\n                bmp[int(r) - r0, int(c) - c0] = True\\n        return np.array_equal(bmp, bmp[:, ::-1])\\n\\n    frame_parts: list[tuple[int, np.ndarray]] = []\\n    for i, (c1, p1) in enumerate(comps8_all):\\n        r0, c0, r1, c1b = bbox_coords(p1)\\n        if r1 - r0 + 1 == H and c1b - c0 + 1 == W:\\n            if _is_lr_symmetric([(c1, p1)], H, W):\\n                frame_parts = [(c1, p1)]\\n                break\\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(comps8_all)):\\n            c2, p2 = comps8_all[j]\\n            union = np.concatenate([p1, p2])\\n            ur0, uc0, ur1, uc1 = bbox_coords(union)\\n            if ur1 - ur0 + 1 == H and uc1 - uc0 + 1 == W:\\n                if _is_lr_symmetric([(c1, p1), (c2, p2)], H, W):\\n                    frame_parts = [(c1, p1), (c2, p2)]\\n                    break\\n        if frame_parts:\\n            break\\n    assert frame_parts, \\\"input must contain 1-2 LR-symmetric pieces (8-conn) whose union bbox equals output dims\\\"\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1967842053/9/codegen_prompts/1.md:87:Assemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1967842053/9/codegen_prompts/1.md:99:Key insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1967842053/9/codegen_prompts/1.md:115:    \"jigsaw\",\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1967842053/9/codegen_prompts/1.md:210:    \"jigsaw\",\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed287049989/3/codegen_prompts/4.md:235:- The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1967842053/9/codegen_prompts/6.md:11:<new_puzzle index=\"6\">\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed56627177/_codegen_logs/run_20260618_210621_gpt54_high_missing/1__codegen_prompts__2.log:28:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed56627177/1/prompt.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\\n\\n每个参考题描述包含：\\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\\n\\n参考题目如下：\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"1\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"5ecac7f7\\\" lines=\\\"23-36\\\">\\n<description>\\nCombine the left, middle, and right ordered objects from three separated panels.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The grid is three HxH panels separated by one-cell vertical separator bars.\\n- The background is the dominant color, and each panel contains exactly three same-color 4-connected foreground objects.\\n\\nRule\\n- Build a new HxH background canvas.\\n- From the left panel copy its leftmost object, from the middle panel copy its middle object, and from the right panel copy its rightmost object.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The separator bars create three aligned layers, and the panel's own left-to-right object order selects which object survives in the merged output.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"2\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"878187ab\\\" lines=\\\"10-24\\\">\\n<description>\\nReplace the scattered foreground markers with a bottom-left count rectangle whose two corner diagonals encode the marker counts.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- A background grid with exactly two foreground colors used as scattered marker pixels.\\n- The two foreground colors have distinct pixel counts.\\n\\nRule\\n- Ignore marker positions and use only the two foreground counts.\\n- Make a 16-by-16 background canvas, fill a bottom-left red rectangle whose height is the smaller count and width is the larger count.\\n- Paint yellow pixels along the two rising 45-degree diagonals from the rectangle's bottom-left and bottom-right corners.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The marker colors and positions vary between examples; their counts alone determine the output rectangle dimensions.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"3\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"981571dc\\\" lines=\\\"6-19\\\">\\n<description>\\nReconstruct a 4-way symmetric mosaic by filling missing (black) pixels.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- A colored grid exhibiting bilateral symmetry along both a vertical and a horizontal axis.\\n- Some pixels are erased to color 0 (black), creating gaps in the pattern.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Infer the vertical and horizontal reflection axes from the visible (non-zero) pixels.\\n- For each black pixel, recover its color from the mirrored position across either axis or both.\\n- When multiple mirrors yield candidates, use the majority color.\\n\\nKey insight: The reflection axes are not given — they must be deduced from the surviving pixels by finding the axes that best preserve the existing symmetry.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"4\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"f560132c\\\" lines=\\\"259-273\\\">\\n<description>\\nAssemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = most common color.\\n- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the \\\"pieces\\\"), scattered across the grid.\\n- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.\\n- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.\\n- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.\\n\\nKey insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"5\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"5a719d11\\\" lines=\\\"44-59\\\">\\n<description>\\nWithin each horizontal pair of two-color panels, swap the motif masks and recolor each pasted mask with the source panel's base color.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- A separator color forms full blank rows and at least one full blank column.\\n- The non-separator area is split into row bands, each containing exactly two same-shaped rectangular panels.\\n- Each panel has a dominant base color and one minority motif color.\\n\\nRule:\\n- For every row band, clear each panel to its own base color.\\n- Paste the right panel's motif mask into the left panel using the right base color.\\n- Paste the left panel's motif mask into the right panel using the left base color.\\n\\nKey insight:\\n- The motif colors themselves are distractors; only each motif's shape mask and the two panel base colors are exchanged.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"6\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"c9680e90\\\" lines=\\\"52-67\\\">\\n<description>\\nMove each mirrored bottom source pixel to the far end of its trail and redraw the top half as its reflection.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- A solid non-background row separates equal-height top and bottom halves.\\n- The top half has one marker color on the background.\\n- In the bottom half, exactly one color has positions that mirror the top markers across the separator, and the other color forms trails touching those source pixels.\\n\\nRule\\n- Identify the bottom source color by reflecting each bottom color into the top half and matching the top marker set.\\n- For every bottom source pixel, follow its unique 4-connected trail of the other bottom color to the trail endpoint.\\n- Clear the old top markers, bottom sources, and trails; place the source color at each endpoint and place the top marker color at the reflected endpoint.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The top half is only a mirror display of the bottom moving sources; the actual motion is encoded by the trail attached to each bottom source pixel.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"7\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"981add89\\\" lines=\\\"10-29\\\">\\n<description>\\nExtend each top-row control pixel downward as a vertical toggle ray in its own color.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- The background is the dominant color.\\n- Non-background pixels on the top row are control pixels, each identifying\\n  one column and one ray color.\\n- Foreground regions below may intersect these control columns.\\n\\nRule:\\n- For every top-row control pixel, process the cells below it in the same\\n  column down to the bottom edge.\\n- A cell that already has the control color is erased to the background.\\n- Every other cell on that downward ray is painted with the control color.\\n- The top-row control pixels themselves remain unchanged.\\n\\nKey insight:\\nThe top row is a set of vertical ray launchers; each ray toggles only cells\\nmatching its own color and overwrites all other colors along that column.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"8\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"da515329\\\" lines=\\\"11-24\\\">\\n<description>\\nGrow four clockwise spiral wall paths from the tips of a teal cross.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = 0 (black). A single teal (8) cross centered in the grid.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Each arm tip launches a \\\"turtle\\\" heading 90° right of its outward direction.\\n- Movement priority: turn right > go straight > turn left (left only if not on border).\\n- Normal step = 2 cells; step right after a turn = 1 cell.\\n- Each new cell must have a 1-cell orthogonal gap from all existing walls except the predecessor.\\n\\nKey insight: The right-turn preference plus alternating 2/1 step lengths creates self-avoiding spirals that tile the grid with uniform spacing.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"9\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"14b8e18c\\\" lines=\\\"40-57\\\">\\n<description>\\nMark every complete square object with red pixels just outside its corners.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- A uniform background contains one or more monochrome 4-connected foreground objects.\\n- The relevant objects are square frames or filled squares: their bbox is square\\n  and all cells on the bbox border have the object's color.\\n- Non-square bars and incomplete fragments may also appear and are ignored.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Keep the input unchanged.\\n- For each complete square object, paint red on the two outside background\\n  pixels orthogonally adjacent to each of the square's four corners.\\n\\nKey insight: The transformation is not based on size or color; it selects\\nobjects whose bounding box is a complete square border and labels only their\\nexposed outer corner-adjacent cells.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"10\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"42f83767\\\" lines=\\\"23-39\\\">\\n<description>\\nExpand the lower color grid by replacing each pixel with its matching top stencil.\\n\\nInput structure\\nThe input has a top legend and a lower dense color grid, separated by blank rows.\\nThe legend contains left-to-right 2x2 color swatches and, to their right,\\nthe same number of square neutral-color stencil masks.\\n\\nRule\\nPair the swatches and stencil masks by left-to-right order. For every pixel in\\nthe lower color grid, paste the paired stencil mask as one output block,\\nrecoloring the mask pixels with that pixel's color and leaving mask holes as bg.\\n\\nKey insight\\nThe lower grid is not copied directly; it is an index grid whose colors select\\nthe stencil shape from the legend, so each input pixel becomes one square block.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"11\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"f3e14006\\\" lines=\\\"21-39\\\">\\n<description>\\nBuild the woven rectangle determined by the two marked cells on each crossed bar.\\n\\nInput structure\\nA black-background grid contains one long horizontal bar and one long vertical bar.\\nEach bar has a dominant base color and exactly two same-colored marker pixels.\\n\\nRule\\nThe horizontal marker pair gives the output column span.  From the horizontal bar\\nrow, walk toward the farther vertical marker; rows and columns alternate every\\nother cell.  Even step/even offset cells use the horizontal marker color until\\nthe nearer vertical marker is reached, then use the vertical marker color.\\nOdd row steps use the horizontal base on marker columns and the original\\ncrossing color on spacer columns.  Spacer columns on even row steps use the\\nvertical base, except at the switch row where they use the crossing color.\\n\\nKey insight\\nThe original cross cell records which base color wins at base/base lattice\\ncrossings; the nearer vertical marker is the color-switch boundary.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"12\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"1478ab18\\\" lines=\\\"35-43\\\">\\n<description>\\nPaint the teal outline of the right triangle determined by the missing corner of the grey-marker square.\\n\\nInput structure\\nThe grid is an orange background with exactly four foreground marker pixels of one color. The markers have a square bounding box: three markers are bbox corners, and the fourth marker is inside that square.\\nRule\\nFind the one corner of the marker bounding square that has no marker. That missing corner is the right-angle vertex of a right isosceles triangle; paint its two axis-aligned legs and the opposite diagonal in teal, leaving existing marker pixels unchanged.\\nKey insight\\nThe present corner marker opposite the missing corner is a decoy; the three triangle vertices are the missing corner and the two marker corners adjacent to it.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"13\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"66ac4c3b\\\" lines=\\\"57-72\\\">\\n<description>\\nComplete the sparse-color reflected copy of the foreground motif across its matching marker row or column.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The grid has one background color and two foreground colors.\\n- One foreground color appears only as a sparse marker row or column.\\n- The other foreground color contains the same sparse marker positions on a parallel row or column, plus the motif on the far side of the sparse markers.\\n\\nRule\\n- Keep the original grid unchanged.\\n- Reflect every motif pixel of the larger foreground color across the two parallel marker lines.\\n- Paint the reflected motif pixels with the sparse marker color.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The two equal marker rows or columns are paired landmarks: the sparse-color marker line borders the source motif, and the matching source-color marker line anchors the reflected copy.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"14\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"4a21e3da\\\" lines=\\\"8-20\\\">\\n<description>\\nCut a sprite along border-control rays and slide pieces to grid corners.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg, fg (main object, ~centered), ctrl (1-2 pixels on grid edges).\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1967842053/9/codegen_prompts/5.md:309:  \"dst\": \"fit against the matching edge d of a jigsaw growing from a corner square tagged by a marker\",\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code